# Line endings are normalised to LF everywhere, in the repository and in the
# working tree, on every platform.
#
# Without this, Git for Windows checks files out as CRLF (its default
# core.autocrlf=true), so what a collaborator has on disk depends on their Git
# configuration rather than on the repository. For a research artifact whose
# whole claim is reproducibility, "the files differ depending on your machine"
# is a bad starting point -- and the generated documents under docs/carlaair/
# are written LF by their generator regardless.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit for the file types this project actually contains, so the intent
# survives someone changing the line above.
*.py    text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.jsonl text eol=lf

# PowerShell is the exception: some Windows tooling is unhappy with LF-only
# .ps1 files, and these are run by Windows, not by the experiment.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# Never touched: treating these as text would corrupt them.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.whl   binary
